INSERT INTO public.manual_chapters (slug, part, position, number_label, title, subtitle, truth, confidentiality, body, draft_status, pull_quote, provenance_note)
VALUES ('the-laboratory-and-the-ground', 'saga', 93, 'Chapter 93', 'The Laboratory and the Ground Under Our Feet', 'A governance laboratory considered, recorded, and set aside for SiteBMS', 'DECISION', 'C2',
$body$It began with a question about whether to pause and build a laboratory.

The thought was natural enough. Before inventing the governance layer of the Human Blockchain — SAS B — out of imagination, why not experience the systems that have spent a decade on collective human coordination: Decidim, Loomio, DAOhaus, Aragon, Open Collective? Enter each as Administrator, assume every role, create an assembly, run a vote, and see where their models break against ours. The insight under it was sharp: the gap may not be governance; the gap may be the roster. (HYPOTHESIS)

A three-model dialogue formed around it. Manus was sent to research open-source DAO management platforms. Lovable pushed back on the hosting assumption: these platforms are Ruby, GraphQL and Solidity stacks, and turning a web project into a container farm for them would bury the learning under dependency work. It proposed instead a separate Observation Lab — live target platforms hosted where they natively run, and a Lovable workbench holding scenarios, a role switcher and an observation ledger. ChatGPT then corrected Lovable twice. Its broad claims about what DAOs and civic platforms "assume" were provocations, not facts. And Decidim, not Loomio, may deserve to be Platform 01: its seeded install separates System Administrator, Organization Administrator and User, hosts isolated organizations, and carries Assemblies, Authorizations, Proposals and Accountability — a sequence that rhymes with our own Need, Proposal, Decision, Assignment, Work, Evidence, Result, Done. (ASSERTION — from vendor documentation, not yet experienced.) ChatGPT also asked that first observations stay neutral — what happened, what surprised, what frustrated, what to steal — before any comparison to Prepare America.

It was a good exchange. The founder's ruling on it was short. (DECISION)

Record it, and breeze right past it.

The answers to governance will present themselves as we work through the requirements of getting SiteBMS off the ground. We do not need a detour through participatory democracy software or token voting to learn how people in this trade coordinate. We already have a system, a set of contracts, pay agreements and workflows that we know work in the disconnected, human, online-and-offline environment of restoration. (ASSERTION — the founder's operating history; the documents themselves enter the corpus through the intake lane.)

What the conversation leaves behind is worth keeping. The distinction between platforms we experience and a workbench that records what we learn. The neutral observation order. The idea that the DH Method itself — find a working example, experience it, assume every role, compare, then write the requirement — could one day have its own instrument. All of it is parked as a sister-project possibility aligned with SAS B, with no work scheduled. (OPEN)

The locked order does not move: Draft Connecticut Agreement, Records and Object Model, SiteBMS, JobNimbus mapping, API and MCP. Governance will be read off the real obligations of real people doing real work, not designed ahead of them.$body$,
